WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.240
Music

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:38.600
In the great tree of life, one branch of the mammals has a particular fascination for us,

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.600
for we belong to it.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.640
Primates.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:01:00.720
Primate hands provide a firm grip, and forward-facing eyes accurately assess distance.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:10.080
Both are crucial for a lifestyle that began in the trees.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:22.480
Intelligence among primates can excel that of all other animals.

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:36.120
Primates can solve difficult problems, develop thoughts and ideas,

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:41.640
and build long-lasting relationships.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:53.200
But most importantly, primates remember what they learned during their lives.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:02:14.560
This film reveals how intelligence helps our closest relatives to tackle the many challenges of life.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:34.360
There are more than 350 primate species on Earth.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:47.760
Since they first appeared over 65 million years ago, these clever animals have become

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:57.280
adapted to an extraordinary range of habitats.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:02.320
The Oash region of southern Ethiopia.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:15.520
The harsh, remote scrubland.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:31.200
And primates have learned how to make it their home.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:40.960
Hamadryas baboons are waking up from a night spent high on the cliffs.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:48.280
These monkeys live in groups up to 400 strong, with no single leader.

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:54.440
Theirs is a very complex society, made up of dozens of small harems, each governed by

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:58.640
a male.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:13.200
Every morning they leave the safety of the cliffs to find food.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:18.440
Top-ranking males lead the way.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:40.520
They can be very severe with their females if there is the slightest misdemeanor.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:47.320
Despite their individual strength, Hamadryas baboons prefer to travel as a troop.

00:04:47.320 --> 00:05:01.520
It's safer that way.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:15.200
On this particular morning, something stops them in their tracks.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:29.680
Another troop is using these cliffs as a barracks.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:45.320
More than a thousand baboons can overnight here, and a rival faction is heading directly

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:53.560
their way.

00:05:53.560 --> 00:06:17.120
In the chaos of warfare, males settle old scores.

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:34.720
This is also their best chance of stealing females.

00:06:34.720 --> 00:07:01.640
When the fighting is over, the harems reform.

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:19.720
Did males punish any of their females who had dared to stray?

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:35.960
Strict discipline is essential if order is to be maintained.

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:52.160
It's a harsh social system, but it works for Hamadryas baboons here on these arid plains.

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:57.760
In other parts of the world, primates have had to organize the societies in a different

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:06.960
way to cope with different challenges.

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:14.040
Midwinter in the Japanese Alps.

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:26.880
The Kamikotchi Valley is the haunt of the most northerly dwelling monkeys.

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:44.760
To live here, you must be able to survive temperatures which plunge to minus 20 centigrade.

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:58.000
Japanese macaque.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:09.600
Dense thick layers of fur help to insulate these snow monkeys in this testing environment.

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:19.960
But they still feel the cold.

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:27.560
At this time of the year, food is scarce.

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:36.960
But macaques are adaptable and clever.

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:47.440
The troop has learned that this river stays ice-free the year round.

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:52.600
This female knows it's a good place to gather insect larvae from under the rocks using her

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:58.240
versatile hands and nimble fingers.

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:15.520
In winter, this troop spends most of its time searching for food.

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:29.160
The same underground forces that prevent this river from freezing bring great comfort to

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:30.160
others.

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:32.320
The Japanese Alps were built by volcanoes.

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:36.160
Many of them are still active.

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:48.800
And in a region called Hell's Valley, some snow monkeys have found the perfect winter

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:49.800
resort.

00:10:49.800 --> 00:11:19.760
A thermal spa where the water temperature is a blissful 41 degrees centigrade.

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:36.120
Everyone wants in, but primates being primates, there are poolside politics.

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:42.440
This is an exclusive members-only club.

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:50.840
Only the highest ranking females and infants are allowed in.

00:11:50.840 --> 00:12:07.440
Everyone obeys this male who guards the pool and vets the entrance.

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:19.720
These youngsters, born of the right bloodline, don't know how privileged they are.

00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:31.560
Lower-ranking individuals are literally left out in the cold.

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:37.560
Japanese macaque society is very divided.

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:47.200
There are those that have and those that have not.

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:59.400
And that is a harsh division because the 60 degrees that separate the steaming water from

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:11.400
the freezing surroundings can make the difference between life and death.

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:29.200
But by far the majority of primate species live in warm, tropical forests.

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:39.160
Among them are the largest of all.

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:46.800
Gorillas live in stable family groups with just a single leader.

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:58.400
A silverback male.

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:03.920
This one, here in the Congo Basin, is the guardian of his family, which includes five

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:15.240
females and their infants.

00:14:15.240 --> 00:14:20.560
He has the responsibility of protecting them from the dangers that abound on the forest

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:30.960
floor where they feed.

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:39.960
To sustain his huge size, he must consume up to 30 kilos a day.

00:14:39.960 --> 00:14:51.760
It's mostly plant food, but Western gorillas also enjoy a sprinkling of termites.

00:14:51.760 --> 00:15:04.080
The youngsters need to eat far less than their father, so they've got time on their hands.

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:13.480
They like to play for the same reasons we do, for fun, and it helps build long lasting relationships.

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:27.400
Their protector keeps a watchful eye on them.

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:49.120
And then it's time for his siesta.

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:58.720
Something shatters the peace.

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:03.600
This silverback's territory is one of the best, but it has borders with at least eight

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:20.160
other gorilla groups.

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:35.840
The sound of chest beating travels more than a mile through the tangled understory.

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:42.560
It's a territorial drumbeat.

00:16:42.560 --> 00:17:00.100
Everyone must know who is boss around here.

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:06.240
Other smaller primates are rather more secretive.

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:16.000
And the most unusual is found in Sulawesi, Indonesia.

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:29.320
It lives among the aerial roots of this strangler fig.

00:17:29.320 --> 00:17:34.600
Its ancestors were daytime hunters, but they found that there was less competition if they

00:17:34.600 --> 00:17:47.400
looked for food in the twilight.

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:54.440
Now they only stir after dark.

00:17:54.440 --> 00:18:19.760
Nonetheless, there's a lot of insect food around.

00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:29.280
The spectral tarsier.

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:37.280
Tarsiers are the only totally carnivorous primates on earth.

00:18:37.280 --> 00:18:43.480
They've hardly changed in 45 million years.

00:18:43.480 --> 00:18:53.040
Their huge eyes can see in even the faintest light.

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:58.840
These eyeballs are so wide they can't swivel in their sockets.

00:18:58.840 --> 00:19:04.480
Tarsiers have to rotate their heads.

00:19:04.480 --> 00:19:10.240
Their mobile ears can detect the faintest sounds.

00:19:10.240 --> 00:19:22.320
And their powerful legs enable them to jump 40 times their own length.

00:19:22.320 --> 00:19:30.560
The family group fans out to hunt.

00:19:30.560 --> 00:19:59.120
prey is not hard to find.

00:19:59.120 --> 00:20:02.920
The tarsiers must be watchful.

00:20:02.920 --> 00:20:13.480
For a primate just five inches tall, life in the forest is full of danger.

00:20:13.480 --> 00:20:19.880
The male sounds the alarm.

00:20:19.880 --> 00:20:29.920
And everyone retreats.

00:20:49.880 --> 00:21:07.200
Back in their family tree, the senior male and female send out piercing calls.

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:18.040
These calls guide in a straggler's home and there they renew the family bonds.

00:21:18.040 --> 00:21:31.000
Native communication is one of the hallmarks of primate society.

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:55.200
Who communicate more musically than La Gibbons in the forests of Thailand?

00:21:55.200 --> 00:22:00.960
Their songs carry for many miles across the canopy, proclaiming that this piece of forest

00:22:00.960 --> 00:22:01.960
is theirs.

00:22:01.960 --> 00:22:20.720
The

00:22:31.960 --> 00:22:45.160
Most primates have excellent colour vision, and colour too can be used in communication.

00:22:45.160 --> 00:23:03.160
Faire's leaf monkeys might seem rather drab, but not so their newborn.

00:23:03.160 --> 00:23:14.160
Bright orange fur makes the babies very conspicuous, so the adults can easily keep an eye on them.

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:21.160
Close friends and relatives are eager to help the mother with babysitting.

00:23:21.160 --> 00:23:32.160
It's a good chance for the younger ones to practice parenting.

00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:43.160
As a result, the baby is never left on its own for long.

00:23:43.160 --> 00:23:57.160
When it's a few months old and more independent, it will turn the colour of its mother and blend in with the group.

00:23:57.160 --> 00:24:16.160
But until then, it can't be ignored.

00:24:16.160 --> 00:24:22.160
Smell is of particular importance to the primates that live in Madagascar.

00:24:22.160 --> 00:24:26.160
The lemurs.

00:24:26.160 --> 00:24:33.160
They have pointed snouts and wet noses.

00:24:33.160 --> 00:24:48.160
These are ring-tailed lemurs.

00:24:48.160 --> 00:24:59.160
The males have sharp pads on their wrists with which they scratch the trunks of young trees.

00:24:59.160 --> 00:25:09.160
Glans on their wrists impregnate the cut bark with a pungent smell that acts as a territorial marker.

00:25:09.160 --> 00:25:14.160
Females make smelly marks in their own way.

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:20.160
This one's scent carries another signal as well as the territorial one.

00:25:20.160 --> 00:25:28.160
It tells males that she's coming into heat.

00:25:28.160 --> 00:25:39.160
But she will only be sexually receptive for 24 hours or less.

00:25:39.160 --> 00:26:04.160
So tensions run high among the males.

00:26:04.160 --> 00:26:10.160
Amid the commotion, some males sneak off.

00:26:10.160 --> 00:26:19.160
They have anointed their tails with scent and wafted towards her in an attempt to persuade her to mate.

00:26:19.160 --> 00:26:33.160
She's ready, but fussy.

00:26:33.160 --> 00:26:49.160
This male adds more of his wrist-glammed perfume to his tail.

00:26:49.160 --> 00:26:58.160
It seems to work, for they leave the party together.

00:26:58.160 --> 00:27:27.160
And he wafts his way to victory.

00:27:27.160 --> 00:27:34.160
A willingness to mate is a relatively straightforward message.

00:27:34.160 --> 00:27:45.160
But primates are capable of much more complex communication.

00:27:45.160 --> 00:27:53.160
It starts between a mother and her baby.

00:27:53.160 --> 00:28:05.160
The rainforests of Sumatra.

00:28:05.160 --> 00:28:10.160
This female orangutan is 42 years old.

00:28:10.160 --> 00:28:20.160
Her third child, a six-year-old daughter, is still with her.

00:28:20.160 --> 00:28:27.160
Orang's look after their children for longer than any other primate except ourselves.

00:28:27.160 --> 00:28:39.160
It will take her nine years to teach her youngster everything she needs to know about this complex tree-top world.

00:28:39.160 --> 00:28:52.160
She must learn how to collect ants and termites.

00:28:52.160 --> 00:29:03.160
How to identify at least 200 kinds of edible plants and how to avoid the poisonous ones.

00:29:03.160 --> 00:29:25.160
And how to judge when fruit, like this durian, has ripened to perfection.

00:29:25.160 --> 00:29:37.160
Her child must be able to judge which branches can carry her weight.

00:29:37.160 --> 00:30:02.160
And which insect nests are safe to raid.

00:30:02.160 --> 00:30:28.160
Building up a complete guide to the foods of the forest is a long process.

00:30:28.160 --> 00:30:32.160
Her lessons, of course, aren't limited to food.

00:30:32.160 --> 00:30:38.160
There are other crucial skills she must learn if she is to survive in the tree-tops.

00:30:38.160 --> 00:30:49.160
Building a secure nest in which to spend the night, for example, takes years of practice.

00:30:49.160 --> 00:30:57.160
And this is, of course, a rain forest.

00:30:57.160 --> 00:31:25.160
All Orang's must learn how to make a shelter early in their lives.

00:31:25.160 --> 00:31:43.160
It rains almost every day, so this six-year-old has already had plenty of practice.

00:31:43.160 --> 00:32:03.160
She might live to be 50 years old, and if she too becomes a mother, she'll pass on all this expertise to her own children.

00:32:03.160 --> 00:32:17.160
Remembering your lessons is a vital part of primate life.

00:32:17.160 --> 00:32:33.160
It's not just learning how to exploit your environment. Just as important is knowing when to do so.

00:32:33.160 --> 00:32:51.160
This is Africa's Cape Peninsula. It's the furthest south that monkeys have managed to settle.

00:32:51.160 --> 00:32:57.160
There's a wide range of plant food here, but because the soil is so poor and in nutrients,

00:32:57.160 --> 00:33:15.160
Chakma baboons find it difficult to get the range of sustenance they need, however much they eat.

00:33:15.160 --> 00:33:30.160
So they have had to become quite adventurous in what they will tackle.

00:33:30.160 --> 00:33:46.160
The waters around the Cape are among the richest in the world.

00:33:46.160 --> 00:34:04.160
And the wily baboons have become tidal experts.

00:34:04.160 --> 00:34:27.160
If you rely on the tides to expose your food, you have to work to fairly tight schedules.

00:34:27.160 --> 00:34:49.160
Every two weeks, on the lowest spring tide, there's a chance to collect something really special.

00:34:49.160 --> 00:35:15.160
What they've been waiting for is hidden among the fronds of seaweed.

00:35:15.160 --> 00:35:41.160
Shark eggs.

00:35:41.160 --> 00:35:54.160
Each one is only a tiny mouthful, but there are lots of them and they're so nutritious it's worth the trouble.

00:35:54.160 --> 00:36:03.160
But the baboons can't stay long. The tide is turning.

00:36:03.160 --> 00:36:11.160
So now they switch their attention to the main course of the day, mussels.

00:36:11.160 --> 00:36:23.160
This delicacy is exposed every day by every tide.

00:36:23.160 --> 00:36:37.160
Baboons have powerful jaws and huge canines, ideal for cracking shells.

00:36:37.160 --> 00:36:55.160
Tining is an essential skill if you're to harvest all the food that becomes available at one time or another around a coast.

00:36:55.160 --> 00:37:05.160
Some foods, however, are only available to those who have skillful hands and sharp intelligence.

00:37:05.160 --> 00:37:18.160
On the coast of Costa Rica, among the mangroves, live some of the most intelligent monkeys in the whole of the Americas.

00:37:18.160 --> 00:37:22.160
White-faced capuchins.

00:37:22.160 --> 00:37:29.160
They too have learnt to work the tides.

00:37:29.160 --> 00:37:35.160
They are after clams.

00:37:35.160 --> 00:37:45.160
But capuchins are quite small and don't have the brawn to open such shellfish.

00:37:45.160 --> 00:37:48.160
But they do have the brain.

00:37:48.160 --> 00:37:54.160
And they've devised an ingenious way to solve the problem.

00:37:54.160 --> 00:38:04.160
They hammer the clams.

00:38:04.160 --> 00:38:07.160
They're not trying to crack the shells.

00:38:07.160 --> 00:38:35.160
All this pounding and rolling has another purpose, to tire the muscle with which the clam is holding itself shut.

00:38:35.160 --> 00:38:49.160
Eventually, the clam can hold out no longer.

00:38:49.160 --> 00:39:03.160
And the capuchin gets its reward.

00:39:03.160 --> 00:39:30.160
Trial and error may have been sufficient to solve this particular problem.

00:39:30.160 --> 00:39:57.160
But one of their cousins in Brazil has taken things a step further.

00:39:57.160 --> 00:40:08.160
Brown-tufted capuchins combine manual dexterity with considerable intelligence.

00:40:08.160 --> 00:40:17.160
And they have learned to use tools, hammer stones, with which to open palm nuts.

00:40:17.160 --> 00:40:30.160
Some of the stones are nearly half the weight of the monkey.

00:40:30.160 --> 00:40:39.160
Without a tool, opening these nuts would be an impossible task.

00:40:39.160 --> 00:40:54.160
Tool-using was a major breakthrough in primate evolution.

00:40:54.160 --> 00:41:22.160
And nowhere is it more convincingly displayed than here in the forest of Bossu in Guinea, West Africa.

00:41:22.160 --> 00:41:50.160
Chimpanzees in this small community of 13 individuals use tools in a variety of ways.

00:41:50.160 --> 00:42:15.160
The most delicate is the way they use a twig or leaf stem to dip for ants.

00:42:15.160 --> 00:42:43.160
I dipped a sehrntся weil I cared a lot about bossallecki on my family.

00:42:43.160 --> 00:43:03.920
Some of their skills are unique to this particular group.

00:43:03.920 --> 00:43:10.800
One of these involves stripping a palm leaf frond and using it like a pestle to mash up

00:43:10.800 --> 00:43:33.080
the nutritious palm heart.

00:43:33.080 --> 00:43:36.360
This four-year-old is learning fast.

00:43:36.360 --> 00:43:41.680
She needs to, if chimpanzees haven't learned particular skills by the age of eight, they

00:43:41.680 --> 00:43:52.840
never seem able to acquire them.

00:43:52.840 --> 00:43:59.200
The most impressive skill of all, which involves nimble fingers, hand-eye coordination and

00:43:59.200 --> 00:44:18.560
intelligence is nut cracking.

00:44:18.560 --> 00:44:22.160
Chimpanzees have gone a stage further than capuchins.

00:44:22.160 --> 00:44:27.440
They have learned how to carefully position the nut on an anvil and to judge how much

00:44:27.440 --> 00:44:34.880
force to use in order to crack the shell but not smash the kernel to pieces.

00:44:34.880 --> 00:44:47.640
Their use of tools is both efficient and precise.

00:44:47.640 --> 00:45:06.680
This 11-year-old female has an anvil but can't find a hammer.

00:45:06.680 --> 00:45:27.640
She approaches a male to see if he will lend her his.

00:45:27.640 --> 00:45:34.280
He obliges.

00:45:34.280 --> 00:45:47.800
Chimpanzees can show great kindness and compassion.

00:45:47.800 --> 00:45:56.080
Sharing.

00:45:56.080 --> 00:46:01.080
Experimenting.

00:46:01.080 --> 00:46:08.080
Planning.

00:46:08.080 --> 00:46:17.600
Intelligence.

00:46:17.600 --> 00:46:33.120
Teaching and learning.

00:46:33.120 --> 00:46:45.760
Behavior so characteristic of us higher primates.

00:46:45.760 --> 00:46:53.080
We are the most inventive and innovative of all primates.

00:46:53.080 --> 00:46:57.840
Just one branch of a large and extended family.

00:46:57.840 --> 00:47:05.240
A family which has refined the ability to develop and pass on individual learning to

00:47:05.240 --> 00:47:22.440
the next generation.

00:47:22.440 --> 00:47:36.120
A family which has built on strong bonds between mother and baby.

00:47:36.120 --> 00:48:00.000
A family with which we share so much.

00:48:06.120 --> 00:48:31.880
To film the very best primate behavior, the life team had to use all their primate ingenuity

00:48:31.880 --> 00:48:46.240
and adaptability.

00:48:46.240 --> 00:48:57.600
And in doing so, they discovered an extraordinary affinity with our extended family.

00:48:57.600 --> 00:49:04.960
Especially with the great ape with whom we share almost 99% of our genes.

00:49:04.960 --> 00:49:05.960
The chimpanzee.

00:49:05.960 --> 00:49:08.960
He was completely asleep just then.

00:49:08.960 --> 00:49:13.960
He was just rocking to the side as if he might just fall off the branch.

00:49:13.960 --> 00:49:16.960
You see his lips were twitching like he was in a deep dream.

00:49:16.960 --> 00:49:31.200
It was really beautiful.

00:49:31.200 --> 00:49:37.000
Chimpanzees are our closest relatives.

00:49:37.000 --> 00:49:42.000
Justine Evans spent almost a month with them in the forests of Guinea to film their use

00:49:42.000 --> 00:49:43.000
of tools.

00:49:43.000 --> 00:49:46.000
The chips have disappeared off down there.

00:49:46.000 --> 00:49:51.040
They're going to cross over into another area of forest and hopefully start using some tools.

00:49:51.040 --> 00:49:52.040
Go and use tools.

00:49:52.040 --> 00:49:57.480
That's what we're here for.

00:49:57.480 --> 00:50:00.760
Chimpanzees have to accept you.

00:50:00.760 --> 00:50:08.480
If they don't want to be filmed, they'll simply disappear.

00:50:08.480 --> 00:50:14.000
Justine needed the expert guidance of Tatiana Humle, a primatologist who spent more than

00:50:14.000 --> 00:50:17.240
10 years studying these individuals.

00:50:17.240 --> 00:50:23.760
So when we see them, we just keep calm and don't stare at them straight into the eyes.

00:50:23.760 --> 00:50:26.760
Have you ever had problems with them coming right up and being aggressive?

00:50:26.760 --> 00:50:27.760
No, never.

00:50:27.760 --> 00:50:28.760
Never.

00:50:28.760 --> 00:50:29.760
I mean, it's pretty rare.

00:50:29.760 --> 00:50:33.160
